  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find $k$, if $‖x‖=5\\sqrt{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the norm of the vector $x$ in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(3k)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9{k}^{2}+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{k}^{2}+14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given equation  $‖x‖=5\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain an equation that we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{k}^{2}+14} & =5\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{9{k}^{2}+14})}^{2} & ={(5\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ 9{k}^{2}+14 & =25\\cdot 2 \\\\ 9{k}^{2}+14 & =50 \\\\ 9{k}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
